# loop-cron.crontab.example — ILLUSTRATIVE host-only scheduling sample
#
# This file is documentation, NOT a working install. It is never bundled by
# team-init, never placed on PATH, and never invoked by CI. Copy it, adapt the
# paths/cadence to YOUR host, and install it yourself — or don't, and keep
# driving the loop by hand. `manual` is always the fallback (see below).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this wires
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Loop contract surface already accepts a time-driven trigger:
#
#     trigger:
#       type: schedule        # <- valid enum value (manual | schedule | event)
#       detail: host-driven cadence
#
# A `schedule` trigger only says "this loop is meant to run on a clock". It does
# NOT itself schedule anything — the *host* supplies the clock. This sample shows
# two ways a host operator can supply it. Both are OPT-IN and OS/host specific;
# the framework ships neither enabled.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Option A — the environment's own /loop + ScheduleWakeup (preferred when present)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `/loop` and `ScheduleWakeup` are ENVIRONMENT primitives (provided by the agent
# runtime), not repo scripts — there is no skills/loop/ in this plugin, so the
# framework cannot self-invoke them. When your environment provides them, drive
# the cadence there (e.g. `/loop 30m /shell-team:run ...`) and set the
# loop's contract trigger.type to `schedule` to document the intent. Nothing in
# this repo needs to change.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Option B — an OS scheduler (cron / launchctl) calling a host wrapper
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# If you want an OS-level clock, point cron at a SMALL host-side wrapper YOU own
# (not shipped here) that invokes whatever your loop runs. Example crontab line —
# every night at 02:30, adapt the path and command to your host:
#
#   30 2 * * *  cd /path/to/your/repo && /path/to/your/host-loop-wrapper.sh >> /path/to/your/loop.log 2>&1
#
# macOS launchd equivalent: a StartCalendarInterval LaunchAgent plist that runs
# the same wrapper — same idea, different scheduler.
#
# The wrapper is THIN and REMOVABLE: it is host glue, not framework code. Delete
# the crontab line (or the LaunchAgent) and the loop can be run by hand exactly
# as before — no behavior change inside the repo. (Removing the host clock does
# NOT rewrite any contract file: a contract left at `trigger.type: schedule`
# stays valid and runnable by hand; optionally edit it back to `manual` yourself
# to reflect the new intent.)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Portability caveat (why this ships disabled)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduling is the least portable piece of the outer loop: adopters' hosts
# differ (cron vs launchd vs systemd timers vs a CI scheduler vs an agent
# runtime's /loop), paths differ, and auth/credentials differ. So the framework
# documents the wiring but never enables it. `manual` is the always-available
# baseline; `schedule` + this host glue is a removable opt-in layer on top.
